# dctris.dc - a falling-block puzzle game written in dc, the Unix desk calculator.
#
#   dc dctris.dc
#
# stdin: the seed, then one event number per line (0 tick, 1 left, 2 right,
# 3 rotate, 4 soft drop, 5 hard drop, 9 quit). stdout: ANSI frames. Rules: ../SPEC.md.
#
# dc has a stack, registers with one-character names, arrays attached to the
# registers, strings that can be executed as macros, and conditionals that
# run a register as a macro: `P Q >m` runs m when P < Q, and `P Q <m` runs it
# when P > Q. (The operand order is the opposite of how it reads.) Loops are
# macros that call themselves.
#
# Registers
#   data      s seed  c piece  n next  x y r position/rotation  t ticks
#             S score  L lines  o game over  e frame due  z event
#   temps     u v w  fits(x, y, rotation) -> k     q mask  i bit  a b row/col
#             h flag/count/value  j counter  d rotate done
#   arrays    B board[200]  M masks[piece*4+rot]  C colours  T line scores
#             G side-panel macros by row  N next-piece cell printers
#   macros    see the definitions below; event handlers are registers 0-5.

# ---- tables -----------------------------------------------------------------
240 0:M 17476 1:M 3840 2:M 8738 3:M
102 4:M 102 5:M 102 6:M 102 7:M
114 8:M 610 9:M 624 10:M 562 11:M
54 12:M 1122 13:M 864 14:M 561 15:M
99 16:M 612 17:M 1584 18:M 306 19:M
113 20:M 550 21:M 1136 22:M 802 23:M
116 24:M 1570 25:M 368 26:M 547 27:M
51 0:C 226 1:C 135 2:C 46 3:C 196 4:C 33 5:C 208 6:C
0 0:T 40 1:T 100 2:T 300 3:T 1200 4:T

# ---- printing helpers ---------------------------------------------------------
# A '[' can't appear unbalanced inside a dc string, so ESC [ is printed as bytes.
[27P91P]s^
[l^x[m]P]sR
[l^x[38;5;60m]P]s<
[l^x[38;5;244m]P]s>
[l^x[1m]P]s/
[l^x[K]P10P]s-
[l^x[38;5;237m]P[ ·]PlRx]s"
[l^x[38;5;]Plh1-;Cn[m██]PlRx]s`

# ---- core -------------------------------------------------------------------
# D: Park-Miller step; the new piece goes to n.
[ls16807*2147483647%dss7%sn]sD
# V: push the level.
[lL10/1+]sV

# F: does piece c fit at (u, v) with rotation w? Result in k.
[0sk]sY
[0sh0sk]sX
[lb10*la+;B0!=Y]sI
[li4%lu+sa li4/lv+sb 1sh la0>X la9<X lb0>X lb19<X lh1=I]sg
[lq2%1=g lq2/sq li1+si lq0<f]sf
[lc4*lw+;Msq 0si 1sk lfx]sF

# N: spawn; game over (o=1) if it doesn't fit.
[1so]sP
[3sx0sy0sr0st lxsu lysv lrsw lFx lk0=P]sN

# H: paint the falling piece into the board with value h.
[lh li4/ly+10* li4%lx++ :B]sA
[lq2%1=A lq2/sq li1+si lq0<U]sU
[lc4*lr+;Msq 0si lUx]sH

# K: lock. Paint, compact rows bottom-up (a reads, b writes), zero-fill the top,
# score, then bring in the next piece.
[lh1+sh]sp
[la10*lj+;B0!=p lj1+dsj10>m]sm
[la10*lj+;B lb10*lj+:B lj1+dsj10>E]sE
[0sj lEx lb1-sb]s)
[0sh0sj lmx lh10>) la1-sa la0!>(]s(
[0lj:B lj1+dsj lb1+10*>{]s{
[0sj l{x]s}
[lc1+sh lHx 19sa19sb l(x lb0!>} lb1+;TlVx*lS+sS lLlb1++sL lnsc lDx lNx]sK

# J: step down, or lock.
[ly1+sy]s,
[lxsu ly1+sv lrsw lFx lk1=, lk0=K]sJ

# ---- event handlers 0-5 -------------------------------------------------------------
[0st lJx 1se]s%
[lt1+st lt lVx+ 11!>%]s0
[lx1-sx]s$
[lx1-su lysv lrsw lFx lk1=$ 1se]s1
[lx1+sx]s&
[lx1+su lysv lrsw lFx lk1=& 1se]s2
# rotate: try offsets 0, -1, +1, -2, +2 until one fits
[lxlj+sx lwsr 1sd]s@
[lxlj+su lysv lFx lk1=@]s|
[sj ld0=|]s~
[lr1+4%sw 0sd 0l~x _1l~x 1l~x _2l~x 2l~x 1se]s3
[lJx 1se]s4
[ly1+sy l*x]s+
[lxsu ly1+sv lrsw lFx lk1=+]s*
[l*x lKx 1se]s5

# ---- rendering ----------------------------------------------------------------
[[  ]P] 0:N
[ln1+sh l`x] 1:N
[ln4*;M 2la2-4*lj+^/2% ;Nx lj1+dsj4>']s'

[[   ]Pl<x[╭──]PlRx[ ]Pl>x[next]PlRx[ ]Pl<x[──────╮]PlRx] 0:G
[[   ]Pl<x[│]PlRx[              ]Pl<x[│]PlRx] 1:G
